\subsection{The exact-shape statement}\label{sec:num-shape}

\Cref{thm:main}(ii) replaces $C_fx^{1/2}Y^{3/2}$ by $\Sigma_{\MT}=\sum_{k\le y}f'(k)\MT(x;f(k))$. We evaluated $\Sigma_{\MT}$ for the rows listed in \cref{tab:shape}, together with $\sum_{k\le y}F(k)h(f(k))$, the main term of \cref{prop:single}(ii) summed over the moduli. We report
\[
N_{\MT}=\frac{\Sigma_{\MT}}{x^{1/2}Y^{3/2}},\qquad N_h=\frac{1}{Y^{3/2}}\sum_{k\le y}F(k)h(f(k)).
\]
$\MT(x;q)$ is computed from the factorisation $t=t_0t_1t_2$ of the proof of \cref{prop:MT}. For $t_0\le\sqrt{Z_1}$ the terms are summed directly. For $t_0>\sqrt{Z_1}$ we use $B(u)=u-u^2$ ($u<1$) and the closed forms
\[
A_0\sum_{(t,q)=1}\eta(t)=\frac6{\pi^2}\prod_{p\mid q}\frac{p^2-2}{p^2-1},\qquad A_0\sum_{(t,q)=1}\frac{\eta(t)}{t^2}=\frac{36}{\pi^4}\prod_{p\mid q}\frac{p^2(p^2-2)}{(p^2-1)^2},
\]
which follow from~\eqref{eq:euler} as in \cref{lem:eta}. The first $64$ terms of the subtracted partial sums are evaluated in $80$-digit arithmetic; $A_0$ and $\prod_pa_p$ are evaluated to high precision through the prime zeta function. The infinite series~\eqref{eq:MT} is thus evaluated without truncation, up to floating-point rounding. As an independent check, \cref{prop:identity} was verified with this implementation of $\MT$ against brute-force values of every other term, for $x=3000$ and all $q\le40$ (largest relative discrepancy $\SelfTest$). For $2k+1$ the number of moduli is about $x/2$, so only $x\le10^6$ was treated.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\small
\input{generated/tab_shape}
\caption{Exact-shape comparison: the data $N_{\mathrm{data}}=N(x,r)$, the normalised sum $N_{\MT}$ of the exact main terms, the ratio $V_f/\Sigma_{\MT}$, and the normalised $h$-average $N_h$. Rows with $r\in\{0.005,0.01,0.1,1\}$ are shown; \texttt{reproduction.json} contains all $\ShapeRows$ computed rows.}
\label{tab:shape}
\end{table}

Over all $\ShapeRows$ computed rows, $V_f/\Sigma_{\MT}$ lies in $[\ShapeMin,\ShapeMax]$. At $x=2.56\cdot10^8$ it differs from $1$ by at most $\ShapeDevLargest$. So the exact main term of \cref{thm:main}(ii) accounts for the whole ratio dependence seen in \cref{tab:largest}. This agreement is much better than the proved error term $x^{5/3+\eps}+x^{1/2}Y$. Relative to $x^{1/2}Y^{3/2}$, and ignoring the unknown constant and the factor $x^\eps$, that error term is of size about $x^{-1/3}r^{-3/2}$. At $x=2.56\cdot10^8$ this is about $1.6$ for $r=0.01$ and $1.6\cdot10^{-3}$ for $r=1$. The pair-count errors $D_x(hq)$ evidently cancel substantially when summed over $h$, which the proof does not exploit. The quantity $N_h$ approaches $C_f$ as $y$ grows, as \cref{prop:poly}(a) predicts. For $k^3+2$ the convergence is slower and less regular, because $y$ is only a few hundred and the error is of order $y^{-1/2}$. The difference $N_{\MT}-N_h$ is the secondary term of the sawtooth sums. It increases with $r$ and decreases as $r$ decreases, consistent with the bound $(Y/x)^{1/6}$ of \cref{prop:MT}, though it appears to behave roughly like a multiple of $\sqrt r$ (\cref{fig:shape}).

